From 15a411c43f80d917d1e1e954f32bbc38cca4b562 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 12:01:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] board/qemu/microblazeel-mmu: fix the qemu command for QEMU >=
 11

QEMU 11 no longer installs a qemu-system-microblazeel binary.  The
MicroBlaze targets were unified: qemu-system-microblaze now runs both
endiannesses, selected by a machine-level "endianness" property that
defaults to big.

That breaks the little-endian board twice over.  The command in this
readme -- which post-image.sh copies into start-qemu.sh and the CI
parses -- names a binary that does not exist, and anyone who falls back
to the unified binary boots the little-endian image on a big-endian
machine, which dies immediately:

  qemu: fatal: Microblaze: unaligned PC=90000d1a

The big-endian board is unaffected because its readme already invokes
qemu-system-microblaze, whose default endianness is big.

Use the unified binary with endianness=little.  Verified with QEMU
11.0.2: the defconfig image boots to the Buildroot login prompt, and
the same image dies with the fatal above when the property is omitted.

Note for users of older QEMU: the endianness property only exists in
unified-target builds; with a QEMU that still ships
qemu-system-microblazeel, the previous command continues to work.

Reported-by: Neal Frager <neal.frager@amd.com>
Signed-off-by: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude (Anthropic)
---
 board/qemu/microblazeel-mmu/readme.txt | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/board/qemu/microblazeel-mmu/readme.txt b/board/qemu/microblazeel-mmu/readme.txt
index 20fa0072..4d1e6606 100644
--- a/board/qemu/microblazeel-mmu/readme.txt
+++ b/board/qemu/microblazeel-mmu/readme.txt
@@ -1,5 +1,5 @@
 Run the emulation with:
 
- qemu-system-microblazeel -M petalogix-s3adsp1800 -kernel output/images/linux.bin -serial stdio # qemu_microblazeel_mmu_defconfig
+ qemu-system-microblaze -M petalogix-s3adsp1800,endianness=little -kernel output/images/linux.bin -serial stdio # qemu_microblazeel_mmu_defconfig
 
 The login prompt will appear in the terminal that started Qemu.
-- 
2.39.5

